  "Source": "N. J. Harrick and A. F. Turner, \"A Thin Film Optical Cavity to Induce Absorption or Thermal Emission,\" Appl. Opt. 9, 2111-2114 (1970). doi:10.1364/AO.9.002111 - Table IV (s design), p. 2113; Fig. 4",
  "Notes": "Cavity inducing unit s-polarized thermal emittance Es = 1 - Rs = 1 from a gold emitter at 5.5 um\nSpecification: Es = 1 - Rs = 1 at 5.5 um, alpha = 28.8 deg in the Si hemicylinder.\nReported: Fig. 4 (p. 2113): s-polarized emittance Es = 1 - Rs vs angle of emission (0-40 deg) at 5.0, 5.5 and 6.0 um. Read from the plot (approximate): 5.5 um peak ~1.0 near 28-29 deg; 6 um peak ~0.97 near 26-27 deg; 5 um peak ~0.9 near 30-31 deg; peaks narrow (FWHM ~2-3 deg) and Es near 0 below ~20 deg. Text: peaks well separated, resolving power in emission ~0.5 um.\nNotes: Table IV s design h/lambda at 5.5 um: cavity 0.113 -> 621.5 nm, FTR 0.240 -> 1320 nm. Reversed from the paper's emitter-first list. The 5.0 and 6.0 um curves of Fig. 4 presumably keep the 5.5 um gold index (only that value is given); dispersion of gold not stated. Peak positions above are read off the plot and are only approximate.\nLayers from Table IV (s design), p. 2113; Fig. 4; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours Rp 0.987, Rs 0.001 at 28.8 deg, 5500 nm; paper Rs 0 (the design condition, Table IV).",
